      {
        "url": "https://icml.cc/virtual/2025/poster/45170",
        "title": "An Autonomous LLM-Agent for Repository-Level Code Auditing",
        "description": "# RepoAudit: An Autonomous LLM-Agent for Repository-Level Code Auditing\n### 2. Preliminaries\nNext, we illustrate the limitations of the LLMs in this task.\n\nA simplified code snippet from the project sofa-pbrpc contains a real NPD bug found by REPOAUDIT.\n\nWhile several bug types, such as API misuse ( [Li et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-23), [2021](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-23)), require only localized reasoning upon abstract syntax trees (ASTs) and are relatively easy to detect with highly effective scanners, many critical bug types demand modeling the entire project as a massive graph and reasoning about properties along and across individual paths in that graph.\n\nConsider the detection of Null Pointer Dereference (NPD) as an example. When repeated is false, the value of json at line 4 propagates to the return statement at line 14. Subsequently, in the function parse_msg, the return value of field2json is assigned to the pointer field_json at line 7 and further dereferenced at line 8 of the function parse_msg, which eventually causes an NPD bug.\n\nOur investigation reveals that 19 out of the 25 weakness (76%) categories necessitate global, path-sensitive reasoning of source-sink reachability upon call graphs, DDGs, and control-flow graphs (CFGs), whereas only 6 categories can be effectively identified through localized analysis based on ASTs.\n\nAccording to (Rozi` [ere et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-1), [2023](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-1); [OpenAI](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-25), [2023](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-25)), many foundation models are initially pre-trained on relatively short text or code snippets.\n\nTo validate our speculation, we conducted a controlled experiment where we prompt Claude 3.5 Sonnet with all the five functions shown in Figure 1 to identify the NPD bug, following a methodology similar to a recent study ( [Fang](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-12) [et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-12), [2024](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-12)). This is a controlled experiment because, in practice, we cannot guarantee knowledge of the comprehensive set of functions associated with a bug. The model exhibited substantial hallucinations, reporting that almost all the dereferenced pointers have null values. Even if we improve the prompts by offering several few-shot examples and explanations on how a null pointer dereference bug occurs, the model still hallucinates, producing false positives and incorrect explanations.\n\nOn the bright side, we observe that LLMs can effectively perform basic analyses when the scope is limited. This capability enables us to surpass traditional program analysis methods, which require compilation and struggle to scale efficiently. While traditional auditing tools rely on heavy-weight yet rigorous techniques to achieve these capabilities, skilled human auditors often rely on intuition to handle such challenges within a constrained analysis scope. We observe similar traits in LLMs.\n\nObserve that the program returned by the LLM only contains critical statements relating to the null value propagation, while irrelevant ones are removed, such as the switch statement from line 8 to line 12 in the function field2json in Figure 1.\n\nDetermining the set of memory objects a pointer variable may point to is known as points-to analysis ( [Smaragdakis et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-33), [2015](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-33)), one of the most challenging problems for downstream static analysis, such as DDG construction.\n\nThe model accurately identifies the two possible points-to facts within the function. It even identifies the path constraints that make the corresponding points-to facts hold. In contrast, the symbolic static analyzer SVF ( [Sui and Xue](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-7), [2016](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-7)) computes the points-to facts without path conditions by default due to its inherent limitations in semantic analysis.\n\nObserve that the model can skip the irrelevant statements, such as the switch statement, avoiding exploring a huge number of program paths.\n\n### 4. Evaluation\nAlso, we attempt to detect new bugs within the targeted code repositories.\n\nAs shown in Table [2](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-49), REPOAUDIT successfully detects all the previously reported bugs, and meanwhile, reports 19 new bugs in historic versions, 14 of which have already been fixed in the latest commit. The average cost per project audit is $2.54, with each true bug detection costing $0.95. Hence, REPOAUDIT can effectively detect the bugs upon real-world programs with low time cost and financial cost.\n\nA pair (m, n) in the column New indicates m new bugs detected in the versions used by existing works, n of which still exist in the latest versions.\n\nIt is shown that CoT prompting can only detect one true bug in the single function-level bug detection and 10 true bugs in the multiple-level bug detection when analyzing the functions relating to the true bugs detected by REPOAUDIT.\n\nAmazon CODEGURU supports the detection of NPD and UAF bugs, while it reports 18 false positives with no true positives. Compared with Meta INFER, REPOAUDIT obtains comparable precision while detecting more true bugs.\n\nOverall, REPOAUDIT detects 185 true bugs with the precision of 85.71%.\n\n### 5. Related Work\nA considerable volume of literature has focused on utilizing LLMs for code auditing ( [Zheng et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-57), [2025](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-57); [Zheng et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-58); [Li](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-52) [et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-52), [2024b](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-52); [a](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-51); [Wang et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-36), [2024a](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-36)). Typically, IRIS employs LLMs to pinpoint sensitive values within programs ( [Li et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-52), [2024b](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-52)), aiding traditional analyzers like CODEQL ( [Avgustinov et al.](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-62), [2016](https://bytez.com/docs/icml/45170/paper?_c=eyJ2IjoxLCJyZWxhdGVkIjpbImNvZGUiLCJyZWZlcmVuY2VzIiwiY29uZmVyZW5jZSJdfQ%3D%3D#id-62)) in taint-style bug detection. However, due to the compilation reliance on symbolic analysis, these techniques lack the capacity for IDE-time analysis. REPOAUDIT belongs to the latter category. Additionally, REPOAUDIT outperforms LLMSAN in terms of recall by employing individual function analysis instead of repository-level end-to-end prompting, effectively mitigating hallucinations in the overall analysis.\n\n### 6. Conclusion\nPowered by Claude-3.5-Sonnet, it detects 40 true bugs in 15 real-world benchmark projects, achieving the precision of 78.43% and reproducing all the bugs discovered by existing techniques. It also detects 185 previously unknown bugs in nine high-profile open-source projects, 174 of which have been confirmed or fixed by developers.\n\n### References\nYue Wang, Weishi Wang, Shafiq R. Joty, and Steven C. H. Hoi. Association for Computational Linguistics, 2021. doi: 10.18653/V1/2021. EMNLP-MAIN. 685.\n\nCoRR, abs/2308.12950, 2023. doi: 10.48550/ARXIV.2308.12950.\n\nDaya Guo, Qihao Zhu, Dejian Yang, Zhenda Xie, Kai Dong, Wentao Zhang, Guanting Chen, Xiao Bi, Yu Wu, YK Li, et al.\n\nShraddha Barke, Michael B James, and Nadia Polikarpova.\n\nJames C. King. ACM, 19(7):385–394, 1976. doi: 10.1145/ 360248.360252.\n\nCristian Cadar, Daniel Dunbar, and Dawson 